                <article id="start" class="guide-section" data-search="onboarding start sign in login register account admin manager cashier location branch demo google gmail apple oauth identity connect provider consent credentials private relay hide email setup callback https">
                    <span class="guide-anchor" id="demo" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    <h2>Getting started</h2>
                    <p class="section-lead">Your workspace brings everyday tasks together. The left menu stays with you, so the next task is always one click away.</p>
                    <div class="quick-start-grid">
                        <a href="{{ route('workspace', ['section' => 'pos']) }}"><strong>I work at the counter</strong><span>Go to Point of sale to sell and print a receipt.</span></a>
                        <a href="{{ route('workspace', ['section' => 'inventory']) }}"><strong>I look after stock</strong><span>Go to Inventory to add, count, and move products.</span></a>
                        <a href="{{ route('workspace', ['section' => 'overview']) }}"><strong>I manage the business</strong><span>Start with Overview, then check Reports and Team.</span></a>
                    </div>
                    <details open><summary>Before your first shift <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Sign in with your own account.</strong><p>Use the email and password your administrator gives you. Personal accounts make activity easier to trace.</p></li><li><strong>Choose the correct location.</strong><p>Check the location selector before selling, adjusting stock, or receiving a delivery. Stock belongs to the location shown.</p></li><li><strong>Check the essentials.</strong><p>Confirm the product price, available stock, and business tax setting. Test your scanner and printer before customers arrive.</p></li></ol></div></details>
                    <div class="guide-note"><p><strong>Exploring the demo?</strong> The sample business contains practice products, customers, and sales. Use the demo to learn the flow. It is separate from a production setup.</p></div>
                    <details><summary>Set up a new business <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><p>Register to create your own business and its Main store. As the administrator, check <strong>Settings</strong> for the tax rate and receipt text, add products and opening stock in <strong>Inventory</strong>, then add staff in <strong>Team</strong>.</p><p>Add a supplier from <strong>Purchasing → Suppliers</strong>. If you select Create order before adding one, POSONLY first asks for your supplier. Add more stores or warehouses from <strong>Settings → Add location</strong>.</p></div></details>
                    <details id="social-sign-in"><summary>Register or sign in with Google or Apple <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Select Continue with Google or Continue with Apple.</strong><p>Use registration for a new workspace, or the login page to return. Complete sign-in and consent on the provider’s own page. Never enter your Google or Apple password into POSONLY.</p></li><li><strong>For a new workspace, finish your business details.</strong><p>Confirm your name, enter your business name, and select Create my workspace. Your administrator account and Main store are created using PHP (Philippine peso). Review tax and receipt details in Settings.</p></li><li><strong>Use the same provider account next time.</strong><p>Select the same Continue button on the login page. If consent is canceled or sign-in expires, start again from login or registration.</p></li></ol><p><strong>Already have a company account?</strong> Sign in using your existing credentials first. Open <strong>Sign-in accounts</strong> from the sidebar. Administrators can also select <strong>Manage sign-in accounts</strong> in Settings. Choose Connect Google or Connect Apple and complete provider sign-in. Your company and role stay the same. Matching email addresses are not automatically merged, and these options do not grant developer access.</p><p>Apple’s Hide My Email can supply a private relay address. Keep using that same Apple account. Recover a forgotten provider password through Google or Apple.</p><p>A <strong>Setup required</strong> button stays unavailable until the operator configures real credentials. Email/password registration and login remain available. Simulated provider identities cannot sign in.</p><a class="text-link" href="{{ route('auth.social.connections') }}">Open connected sign-in accounts</a></div></details>
                    <details id="social-provider-setup"><summary>Operator: configure Google or Apple access <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><p>Add credentials privately to the local <code>.env</code> using the empty variables in <code>.env.example</code>. Keep secrets and Apple key contents out of chat, public folders, browser forms, and committed files. After saving, run <code>php artisan config:clear --no-interaction</code>.</p><p><strong>Google:</strong> configure a Web application OAuth client, its consent screen, and test users as required. Set <code>GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID</code>, <code>GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET</code>, and <code>GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI</code>. Register the exact callback with Google; for this local app it is <code>http://127.0.0.1:8010/auth/google/callback</code>. Host or port changes must be reflected in both places.</p><p><strong>Apple:</strong> configure a Services ID associated with an enabled primary App ID, then set <code>APPLE_CLIENT_ID</code>, <code>APPLE_TEAM_ID</code>, <code>APPLE_KEY_ID</code>, <code>APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH</code>, and <code>APPLE_REDIRECT_URI</code>. The key path points to a private <code>.p8</code> file outside <code>public</code>, <code>storage/app/public</code>, and any configured public storage directory. Register the callback domain and exact return URL with Apple.</p><p>Apple requires a public HTTPS callback and cannot return directly to this app’s <code>127.0.0.1</code> address. Its callback must share the HTTPS origin configured in <code>APP_URL</code>. Open the application through that same registered HTTPS address before starting Apple sign-in so its session can be verified. Leave Apple unavailable until the secure test address is configured. Configuring credentials does not deploy the application.</p><p>Follow the <a href="https://developers.google.com/identity/openid-connect/openid-connect" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google OpenID Connect documentation</a> and <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/signinwithapple/authenticating-users-with-sign-in-with-apple" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Apple Sign in with Apple documentation</a>. Once configured, test consent, new workspace creation, later login, and connecting an existing account.</p></div></details>
                    <p class="section-footnote">If an action is unavailable, your role may not have permission. Ask your administrator to check your access. Cashiers see their own sales and cannot view product costs.</p>
                </article>

                <article id="pos" class="guide-section" data-search="pos wholesale retail held saved cart session float drawer cash count checkout sale payment cash card digital wallet e-wallet split tender barcode scan discount tax receipt change preview print layout printer paper 80mm 72mm scale">
                    <h2>Point of sale</h2>
                    <p class="section-lead">Large product cards, one clear cart, and a payment screen that keeps the total in sight.</p>
                    <details open><summary>Complete a sale <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Open Point of sale and check the location.</strong><p>Choose the store where the sale is happening.</p></li><li><strong>Add the products.</strong><p>Tap a product card, use search, choose a category, or scan its barcode into the search field. Use + or − in the cart to change quantities.</p></li><li><strong>Review the cart.</strong><p>Choose Retail prices or Wholesale prices and check quantities and current prices. Every item needs a saved wholesale price for wholesale checkout. Tag a customer if needed and enter a discount percentage when your store allows it. The configured tax is added after the discount.</p></li><li><strong>Select Charge and choose the payment.</strong><p>Record cash, card, e-wallet, or a split payment. Enter the amount for each tender and check the change shown.</p></li><li><strong>Select Complete sale once.</strong><p>Wait for the success message. The sale is saved and stock is deducted together. In the receipt dialog, optionally select Preview print layout to inspect the same document used by Print receipt without starting the system dialog. Print receipt opens the normal browser print dialog.</p></li></ol><p>Receipt content is at most <strong>72 mm</strong> wide for <strong>80 mm</strong> paper. Choose 80 mm paper in your printer driver and use 100% scale where appropriate. The preview is a layout proof; verify physical output with the actual printer.</p><a class="text-link" href="{{ route('workspace', ['section' => 'pos']) }}">Open Point of sale</a></div></details>
                    <details><summary>Cash, card, e-wallet, and split payments <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><p><strong>Cash:</strong> enter the cash received and confirm the displayed change. <strong>Split:</strong> enter each part of the payment so the combined amount covers the sale.</p><p><strong>Card and e-wallet:</strong> process the payment using your separate provider or terminal, confirm it succeeded, then record the payment in POSONLY. Recording a tender does not charge a bank card or wallet.</p></div></details>
                    <div class="guide-note"><p><strong>Connection interrupted?</strong> Keep this browser tab open. Select <strong>Check sale status</strong> in the payment dialog, or <strong>Check sale</strong> in Point of sale, to retry the protected original checkout. The same tab can recover its original cart and payment after a reload. Do not recreate the checkout while its status is unknown. An internet connection is required; this is not an offline sales queue.</p></div>
                    <details><summary>Save a cart and resume it later <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Select Save cart and give it a name.</strong><p>Keep a recognizable name for the customer or task.</p></li><li><strong>Open Held carts in this browser and account.</strong><p>Select Resume to replace the current cart, or Delete to remove a saved cart.</p></li><li><strong>Review it before charging.</strong><p>Check the location, customer, price tier, current prices, quantities, and stock. Resuming uses the current catalog.</p></li></ol><p>Held carts stay on this browser/device for your account. They reserve no stock, do not sync to another device, and do not allow offline selling. Clearing browser storage removes them.</p></div></details>
                    <details id="cash-session"><summary>Open, count, and close your cash session <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Open Point of sale → Cash session.</strong><p>Check the location, enter the cash already in your drawer as Opening float, and select Open cash session.</p></li><li><strong>Record physical drawer movements.</strong><p>Use Cash in / out, choose a direction, enter the amount, and give a reason. Cash out cannot exceed the expected balance. Record an actual cash refund separately as Cash out.</p></li><li><strong>Close & count at shift end.</strong><p>Count the actual drawer cash, enter Counted cash and an optional count note, then select Close cash session. Review the saved variance.</p></li></ol><p>Expected cash is opening float plus this account’s cash tenders after change, plus cash in, minus cash out. Card and wallet payments do not add drawer cash. A return does not automatically remove cash. Closed sessions keep their saved count and variance; they cannot receive new movements.</p><p>Sessions belong to your account and location. Checkout works without an open session. After an interrupted update, use Check update status in the same tab before starting another cash action.</p></div></details>
                </article>

                <article id="inventory" class="guide-section" data-search="inventory product catalog sku barcode variants family siblings matrix generate combinations batch category price cost stock add edit adjust count transfer size color illustration swatch threshold reorder">
                    <h2>Inventory</h2>
                    <p class="section-lead">Use one product record for each sellable item or variant. A blue medium shirt and a blue large shirt need separate SKUs.</p>
                    <details open><summary>Add a product <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Open Inventory → Add product.</strong><p>Enter a clear name, a unique SKU, and a barcode if you use a scanner.</p></li><li><strong>Set the product details.</strong><p>Add the category, retail price, optional wholesale price, cost, and optional variant description. Use Add variants below for grouped size/color combinations. Set the low-stock threshold to the available quantity at which you want an alert.</p></li><li><strong>Enter opening stock and save.</strong><p>Count the actual units at the selected location. Check the saved item and its stock before selling it.</p></li></ol></div></details>
                    <details id="variants"><summary>Create size/color variants <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Open Inventory → Add variants.</strong><p>Enter the family name, category, default retail/wholesale prices, cost, and reorder level.</p></li><li><strong>Enter Sizes and Colors, separated with commas.</strong><p>Use Size, Color, or both. For example: S, M, L and Blue, Black. Choose an SKU prefix and the opening-stock location.</p></li><li><strong>Select Generate combinations and review each row.</strong><p>Use up to 100 new rows per batch. Edit each unique SKU, optional Barcode, Selling price, and actual Opening stock. Remove combinations you do not sell. Generating again replaces row edits.</p></li><li><strong>Select Create variants once.</strong><p>Every combination becomes a separate sellable SKU with its own stock. Check the saved rows before checkout.</p></li></ol><p><strong>Starting from an existing product?</strong> Its Create variants action prefills the builder. The original SKU and stock remain standalone. Opening quantities are entered separately; stock is not copied.</p><p><strong>Interrupted confirmation?</strong> Use Check update status in the same tab to retry the protected original batch, including after a reload. Do not rebuild it while its result is unknown. A retry cannot create duplicate SKUs.</p></div></details>
                    <details><summary>Manage sibling SKUs and edit their options <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><p>Select <strong>Manage variants</strong> from a family SKU to see its siblings. <strong>Add variants</strong> uses the same fixed option types and skips existing combinations. New SKUs inherit the original family defaults; use Edit on a saved SKU to change its cost, wholesale price, or reorder level.</p><p><strong>Edit</strong> changes that SKU’s Size/Color values and catalog details. Its name is generated from the family name and options. Keep combinations unique. Option values allow up to 30 characters; family names allow 80. <strong>Illustration color</strong> is a visual swatch, separate from the text Color option. Standalone free-text variant descriptions remain supported.</p><p>Choose the exact SKU for checkout, receiving, transfer, replenishment, and Channels. Siblings never share stock or reservations. Original labels and amounts remain on saved receipts and pending quotes after edits; a return restores only the original selected SKU.</p></div></details>
                    <details><summary>Read on-hand, reserved, and available stock <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><p><strong>On hand</strong> is the physical count. <strong>Reserved</strong> is held for pending local online orders. <strong>Available</strong> equals on hand minus reserved.</p><p>10 units on hand minus 8 reserved leaves 2 available to sell or transfer. Low-stock alerts and replenishment targets use available units. Inventory totals and valuation use physical stock.</p></div></details>
                    <details><summary>Correct a count or edit a product <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><p>Use the product’s table menu → <strong>Edit</strong> to change catalog details. Use <strong>Adjust</strong> for a physical count correction, damaged stock, or another stock change.</p><p>Enter the <strong>change in units</strong>, not the final total. If the screen shows 10 units but you count 8, enter <strong>−2</strong>. Add a reason such as “monthly count” or “damaged packaging”. An adjustment is recorded in stock movement history. Physical stock cannot be reduced below reserved units. Ask a manager to review pending online orders before reconciling a shortage.</p><div class="mini-example"><span>CURRENT</span><strong>10 units</strong><span>ADJUSTMENT</span><strong>−2 units</strong><span>NEW STOCK</span><strong>8 units</strong></div></div></details>
                    <details><summary>Move stock between locations <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Select the source location.</strong><p>Open the product menu and choose Transfer.</p></li><li><strong>Choose the destination and quantity.</strong><p>Use a positive number no greater than the available units. Reserved stock cannot be moved. Check that it matches the units physically being moved.</p></li><li><strong>Confirm the transfer.</strong><p>Stock leaves the source and enters the destination together. Check both locations after the move.</p></li></ol><p>Transfers are recorded immediately. There is no in-transit or delivery-confirmation stage in this version.</p></div></details>
                    <div class="guide-note"><p><strong>Stock is checked when a sale completes.</strong> A product visible in a cart is not a reservation. If another staff member sells the last unit first, update the cart when POSONLY asks you to.</p></div>
                    <p class="section-footnote">A visible, idle workspace refreshes every 20 seconds. Use Refresh when you need the latest records sooner. Instant stock push between browsers is not connected.</p>
                </article>

                <article id="purchases" class="guide-section" data-search="purchase purchasing supplier order draft approve cancel restock reorder replenishment target rule low stock alert receive partial delivery history warehouse cost retry connection timeout confirmation">
                    <h2>Purchasing</h2>
                    <p class="section-lead">Review the next order, count each delivery, and keep a clear record of what is still on the way.</p>
                    <details open><summary>Create, approve, and receive an order <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Open Purchasing → Create order.</strong><p>Choose the supplier and receiving location. Add products, quantities, and unit costs.</p></li><li><strong>Choose Draft or Approved.</strong><p>Save as Draft for review, then select Approve order when ready. Approved orders are ready to receive. Neither step adds stock.</p></li><li><strong>Place the order with your supplier.</strong><p>Use your normal process. POSONLY does not send supplier messages.</p></li><li><strong>Count this delivery and select Receive delivery.</strong><p>Enter only the units arriving now; leave undelivered products at zero. Receive all remaining fills the outstanding quantities—use it only when those units arrived.</p></li><li><strong>Review and select Record delivery.</strong><p>Add a delivery note. Each delivery adds its counted units once. The order stays Partially received until all units arrive.</p></li></ol><a class="text-link" href="{{ route('workspace', ['section' => 'purchases']) }}">Open Purchasing</a></div></details>
                    <details><summary>Review delivery history or cancel outstanding units <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><p>Open <strong>View details</strong> to review ordered, received, and remaining units. <strong>Delivery history</strong> shows each receipt, staff member, time, quantities, and note.</p><p>Choose <strong>Cancel outstanding</strong> if the rest will not arrive. Received stock and history stay intact. A fully received order cannot be cancelled, and cancelled orders cannot receive new deliveries.</p></div></details>
                    <details id="replenishment"><summary>Set a replenishment rule and generate drafts <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Select the location, then Replenishment rules → Add rule.</strong><p>Choose the product and supplier. Set a target of available units above the product’s low-stock threshold and enable the rule.</p></li><li><strong>Select Generate drafts.</strong><p>This checks enabled rules across all locations. Background stock checks and the five-minute schedule can also generate drafts.</p></li><li><strong>Review and approve each draft.</strong><p>Generation counts available stock plus remaining units on draft, approved, and partially received orders from all suppliers. It drafts only the units needed to reach the target when available stock is at or below its threshold. Pending online reservations can trigger drafts before import.</p></li></ol><p>Use Edit rule to change the target, or Pause / Enable to control generation. Cancelling an order can allow an enabled rule to draft again; pause the rule too if replenishment should stop. Turning alerts off does not pause rules. Expired subscription write access blocks new drafts while alerts continue.</p></div></details>
                    <div class="guide-note"><p><strong>Order or delivery response interrupted?</strong> Keep the same browser tab and select <strong>Check update status</strong>. Reloading that tab can recover the original order or delivery request. The same request is confirmed once; do not create another order or delivery while its result is unknown. Drafts and approvals send nothing to suppliers.</p></div>
                </article>

                <article id="returns" class="guide-section" data-search="return partial refund sale cancel reverse receipt restock reports customer money original allocation discount tax history">
                    <h2>Returns</h2>
                    <p class="section-lead">Managers and administrators can return selected items or all remaining items. Start with the original receipt.</p>
                    <details open><summary>Return selected items or the remaining sale <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Open Reports and find the sale.</strong><p>Check the receipt number, items, and total.</p></li><li><strong>Select Return and set quantities.</strong><p>Use zero for items you are keeping. Return all remaining selects every unit that has not already been returned. Check that each selected item can go back into saleable stock.</p></li><li><strong>Enter a reason and review the refund preview.</strong><p>The refund uses the original price, discount, tax, and rounding allocations. Current catalog prices do not change it.</p></li><li><strong>Select Record return once.</strong><p>Only selected units return to the original location. Partial returns retain their history; returning all remaining units marks the sale returned. Previous returns cannot be repeated.</p></li><li><strong>Handle the actual refund.</strong><p>Use cash or your external payment provider. POSONLY records the return; it does not transfer money. Record an actual cash refund separately as Cash out in your cash session.</p></li></ol></div></details>
                    <div class="guide-note"><p><strong>Confirmation interrupted?</strong> Use <strong>Check update status</strong> in the same tab to retry the protected original request. A retry cannot restock twice. Damaged-item disposition is not integrated; ask a manager before restoring items that cannot be sold.</p></div>
                </article>

                <article id="customers" class="guide-section" data-search="customer tag contact email phone details customer sale history">
                    <h2>Customers</h2>
                    <details open><summary>Add and tag a customer <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Open Customers and add the customer.</strong><p>Enter their name and any contact details they agree to share. Check for an existing record first.</p></li><li><strong>Tag them at checkout.</strong><p>Choose the customer in Point of sale before completing the sale. A customer is optional; walk-in sales work too.</p></li><li><strong>Review the customer list when needed.</strong><p>Use the available customer information to recognize returning customers and connect their sales.</p></li></ol></div></details>
                    <p class="section-footnote">Customer records do not send marketing messages, create loyalty points, or subscribe anyone to email.</p>
                </article>

                <article id="reports" class="guide-section" data-search="report overview analytics revenue sales best sellers peak hours movement financial export receipt search older history pagination total return dates custom range filter source Manila variant SKU ranking tax margin cost snapshot basis refund cohort coverage tender">
                    <span class="guide-anchor" id="overview" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    <span class="guide-anchor" id="dashboard" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    <h2>Reports</h2>
                    <details open><summary>Review sales and stock activity <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Start with Overview.</strong><p>Check the sales, stock, and low-stock summaries. Use the location selector where shown.</p></li><li><strong>Open Reports for the detail.</strong><p>Choose your filters, then read Busy hours and Your best sellers, by SKU. Open Receipt under Matching receipts &amp; returns to inspect a transaction or print a copy.</p></li><li><strong>Review stock movement history in Inventory.</strong><p>Compare sale, return, adjustment, receipt, and transfer movements when investigating a stock difference.</p></li><li><strong>Reconcile your shift.</strong><p>Compare recorded tenders with counted cash and your external card or wallet reports. Ask your manager to investigate differences before changing stock.</p></li></ol></div></details>
                    <details id="analytics"><summary>Find busy hours and best-selling products <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Choose a date range in Reports.</strong><p>Use Today, Last 7 days, Last 30 days, This month, All time, or Custom range. Custom dates need both From and Through; both dates are included in Manila time.</p></li><li><strong>Choose the location and source.</strong><p>Report location can include All locations. Sales source offers All sources, Point of sale, or Simulated channels. Cashiers see their own sales; managers and administrators see the business's sales.</p></li><li><strong>Choose your ranking and apply it.</strong><p>Rank best sellers by Net units sold or Net recorded sales, then select Apply filters. Refresh report reloads the applied selection. Reset filters restores the initial selection.</p></li><li><strong>Read the pattern, then check the receipts.</strong><p>Busy hours counts receipts in each of the 24 hours across your selected dates. Every hour tied for the highest traffic is a peak; returned receipts still count. Best sellers show up to ten SKUs with positive net units. Each size/color variant ranks separately, using the latest matching receipt's label and SKU.</p></li></ol><p><strong>Returns follow the original sale.</strong> Net recorded sales includes the original discount and tax, less all recorded returns. A later return reduces the original sale's period and hour. Net units means sold units less returned units. Changing current catalog prices never changes old sales.</p><p>Imported channel sales are labeled simulated and process no real payment. Pending or canceled channel orders are not sales. Administrators can select <strong>Export sales CSV</strong> for the full history matching the applied date, location, and source.</p></div></details>
                    <details><summary>Find an older receipt <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Open Reports → Sales analytics.</strong><p>Choose the date range, location, and source, then Apply filters. Use All time if you do not know the sale date.</p></li><li><strong>Find Matching receipts &amp; returns.</strong><p>Enter the full receipt number and select Search, or browse with Previous and Next. Clear search returns to all receipts within the applied filters.</p></li><li><strong>Open Receipt to inspect or print it.</strong><p>Managers and administrators can select Return. Cashiers see only their own sales. The receipt is fetched again so it includes the latest recorded returns.</p></li></ol></div></details>
                    <details id="financial"><summary>Read sales, refunds, tax, and margin estimates <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Open Reports → Financial reports.</strong><p>Administrators and managers can use this tab. Cashiers keep their own Sales analytics.</p></li><li><strong>Choose your dates, location, source, and report basis.</strong><p>Dates include both From and Through in Manila time. Select Apply filters. Each tab keeps its own filters; Refresh report reloads the last applied financial selection.</p></li><li><strong>Read the totals and open the detail you need.</strong><p>Review Net sales excluding tax, Net recorded tax, Recorded refunds, and Total after refunds. Expand Daily movement, By location, By sales source, Tax breakdown, or Original payment records. Administrators can use Export financial CSV for every section with the last successfully applied filters and basis.</p></li></ol><p><strong>Sales &amp; refund dates</strong> is the default: sales follow their sale dates and returns follow their recorded dates. A return-only period can be negative. <strong>Original sale dates</strong> includes all returns for sales made in the selected period, including later returns. For a sale on October 10 returned on October 11, the first basis reports the return on October 11; the second reduces October 10's sales.</p><p><strong>Estimated gross margin needs original costs.</strong> New POS sales capture cost at checkout. New simulated channel orders capture it when quoted and retain it on import. Returns use the original sale cost. Old sales and existing pending orders are not backfilled. Known cost totals cover only units with snapshots; margin stays Unavailable if any included sold or returned units lack costs. Zero cost is valid. Current catalog costs are never substituted, and operating expenses are excluded.</p><p><strong>Original payment records are before refunds.</strong> Cash change is deducted from cash only. Refund payment destinations remain unallocated; reconcile actual refunds, cash movement, and provider settlement separately. Simulated channel records move no real money.</p><p><strong>Check Historical record coverage.</strong> Older returns use their recorded return timestamp when available. Undated returns are disclosed and excluded from Sales &amp; refund dates, including All time; Original sale dates includes them with their sale. Coverage notes for unknown dates follow the selected location and source. These figures do not establish net profit, supplier payments, or statutory tax liability.</p></div></details>
                    <div class="guide-note"><p><strong>Use the right figures.</strong> Analytics and the paged receipt search include the full matching history. Overview charts and customer cards use the latest 500 permitted receipts. These figures describe operations; they do not represent profit, payment settlement, or refunds paid during the selected dates.</p></div>
                    <p class="section-footnote">Full accounting, settlement reconciliation, and statutory tax filing are not integrated. Reconcile actual cash, card, and wallet movement separately.</p>
                </article>

                <article id="team" class="guide-section" data-search="team user invite role permission access edit pause reactivate activity audit log history filter administrator admin manager cashier password security settings tax receipt branding address contact logo png jpeg webp upload print profile alerts business monitoring system health database cache storage worker scheduler heartbeat background check diagnostic incident acknowledge">
                    <h2>Team and settings</h2>
                    <details open><summary>Give staff their own accounts <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Open Team as an administrator.</strong><p>Add the staff member’s name, email, temporary password, and role.</p></li><li><strong>Choose only the access they need.</strong><p>Cashiers handle checkout and customer tasks. Managers handle stock and purchasing as well as sales. Administrators also manage the team, business settings, and sales CSV exports.</p></li><li><strong>Share credentials privately.</strong><p>The Team form creates the account directly. It does not send an invitation email. Give the password to that staff member using your organization’s secure process.</p></li></ol></div></details>
                    <details><summary>Change a role, pause access, or reactivate staff <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Open Team → Edit access.</strong><p>Change the staff member’s name or permission level, review it, and select Save team member. Role changes apply to their next workspace request.</p></li><li><strong>Use Pause access when they should stop working.</strong><p>Review the confirmation and select Pause access. Their sales and activity records stay available.</p></li><li><strong>Use Reactivate when they should return.</strong><p>Confirm Reactivate access. This restores access with their existing role and password; it does not reset their password.</p></li></ol><p>Keep at least one active administrator. Another administrator must change your own role or pause your access. You can edit your own name.</p></div></details>
                    <details id="activity"><summary>Review staff activity <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Open Activity as an administrator.</strong><p>The log covers all locations in your business.</p></li><li><strong>Choose filters and select Apply filters.</strong><p>Filter by team member, action, and From/To dates. Check the time, staff member, action, and record reference.</p></li><li><strong>Browse or refresh the record.</strong><p>Use Previous / Next for other pages, Refresh activity for new entries, or Reset to clear filters.</p></li></ol><p>Only successful workspace actions since logging was enabled are listed. Older history, failed actions, sign-ins, and page views are not backfilled. Repeated checkout, return, or delivery confirmations can appear more than once; this does not mean stock changed twice. The log excludes passwords, payment details, request contents, and recovery tokens.</p><a class="text-link" href="{{ route('workspace', ['section' => 'activity']) }}">Open Activity</a></div></details>
                    <details id="monitoring"><summary>Check system health <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Open System health as an administrator.</strong><p>Select Refresh health to read actual database, cache, private storage, and log-directory checks. Worker and scheduler readings use shared infrastructure heartbeats. Notices and diagnostic records belong to your business.</p></li><li><strong>Run a background check when needed.</strong><p>Select Run background check to confirm a harmless diagnostic job runs, then refresh for the result. An existing pending check is reused; new attempts are limited to one per 60 seconds. Use Check diagnostic status if the request result is uncertain. A long wait stays pending. Ask the operator to check the worker before assuming failure or repeating a business action.</p></li><li><strong>Review and acknowledge a local incident.</strong><p>Investigate the cause, then select Acknowledge incident. This marks the notice as reviewed; it does not repair an error, delete a failed job, or retry one.</p></li></ol><p>Missing or stale heartbeats need investigation. A fresh heartbeat confirms recent activity, not that every job succeeded. A synchronous queue has no separate worker pulse to verify. Recent lists show up to 50 notices and 50 checks. Notices and completed checks use 30-day retention; outstanding pending checks remain visible until completed.</p><p>System health remains available after subscription access expires. It shows sanitized notices without raw logs, exception messages, paths, or request secrets, and sends no email, SMS, or push alerts.</p><a class="text-link" href="{{ route('workspace', ['section' => 'monitoring']) }}">Open System health</a></div></details>
                    <details id="settings"><summary>Check the business settings <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><p>Open <strong>Settings</strong> as an administrator. Set the business’s tax rate, receipt footer, and stock-alert preference, then save. Review the settings before processing live sales.</p><p><strong>Customize company receipts.</strong> Enter your company’s receipt address and contact number. Upload a local PNG, JPEG, or WebP logo up to <strong>1 MB</strong>, with each dimension no larger than <strong>2,000 pixels</strong>, or remove the current logo. Select <strong>Save settings</strong>, then preview or print a receipt.</p><p>These details apply only to your company; only administrators can edit them. The logo is kept in private local storage. Blank optional fields are omitted, and the company name remains its registered name. All receipts use the latest saved profile, including previews and reprints of earlier sales; branding is not frozen at checkout.</p><p>Use Billing &amp; plans for plan, invoice, and access-period management. Check the displayed payment mode before opening hosted checkout: live mode collects real money, while test and local simulations do not. An operator configures payment credentials privately outside this screen. A single configured tax rate is used for checkout. Product-specific tax rules and regional tax engines are not included.</p></div></details>
                    <div class="guide-note"><p><strong>Keep access personal.</strong> Avoid shared cashier logins and sign out at the end of a shift. Contact your administrator if you cannot sign in or need a different role.</p></div>
                </article>

                <article id="billing" class="guide-section" data-search="billing plans subscription trial monthly annual invoice export csv simulation payment limit locations active staff renewal cancellation grace access expiry paymongo hosted checkout test recheck recover dashboard session unknown review webhook credentials">
                    <h2>Billing and plans</h2>
                    <p class="section-lead">Administrators can review plans, invoices, and payment history. @if($paymongoLive)PayMongo live mode collects real payments through hosted checkout.@elseif($paymongoTest)Configured PayMongo test checkout and local simulations collect no real money.@else Available payment options and their mode are shown in Billing &amp; plans.@endif</p>
                    <details open><summary>Choose a plan and review payment <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Open Billing & plans.</strong><p>Review the trial, access dates, locations, and active staff usage.</p></li><li><strong>Choose Monthly or Annual and review the plan.</strong><p>Select Choose this plan. During an active paid period, Schedule this plan applies the change at the next renewal. A plan must fit your current locations and active staff.</p></li><li><strong>Create an invoice when available.</strong><p>Select Create invoice, then View invoice. Review the amount and payment-mode label. Enabled PayMongo checkout opens a hosted payment page; local simulation offers Simulate payment only in a testing environment.</p></li></ol><p>Renewals create individual invoices and do not collect payments automatically. The invoice list shows the latest 100 records; Export invoice CSV includes the full history with provider/mode and safe payment references. Live confirmations, PayMongo test records, and local simulations retain separate source labels. Test and simulated records do not establish a live payment.</p></div></details>
                    <details id="paymongo"><summary>Use PayMongo hosted checkout <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><p>Review the mode displayed on the invoice before paying. Live mode collects real money. Test mode is for local testing and collects no real money. Payment methods shown on the hosted page depend on the operator’s active PayMongo configuration.</p>@if($paymongoMethods !== '')<p><strong>Configured methods:</strong> {{ $paymongoMethods }}. The provider account must also have these methods activated.</p>@endif<ol class="steps"><li><strong>Prepare and open the hosted page.</strong><p>From View invoice, select @if($paymongoLive)Pay with PayMongo (live), then Open PayMongo checkout.@elseif($paymongoTest)Pay with PayMongo (test), then Open PayMongo test checkout.@else the enabled Pay with PayMongo action, then its Open checkout link. If configuration is incomplete, checkout remains disabled.@endif Review the invoice amount before continuing.</p></li><li><strong>Complete payment on PayMongo’s page.</strong><p>@if($paymongoTest)For a successful card test, choose Card and enter Visa <strong>4343434343434345</strong>, an expiry after today, and a three-digit CVC. Use only test details from <a href="https://docs.paymongo.com/docs/payment-acceptance-testing" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PayMongo’s official test guide</a>.@else Follow the hosted page’s instructions for an available payment method. Enter payment details only on PayMongo’s page. Do not use test card details in live mode.@endif</p></li><li><strong>Recheck payment after returning.</strong><p>Use Recheck payment. Only server verification can confirm payment and start the invoice period. A success URL or closing the checkout tab does not prove payment or cancel the invoice. Rechecking a confirmed payment keeps the existing paid-period dates.</p></li><li><strong>Recover an uncertain creation.</strong><p>If the session is known, use Recheck payment. If creation is uncertain and no session ID was saved, do not create it again. Select Recover checkout and enter the Checkout session ID copied from the matching live or test PayMongo dashboard. The server checks the saved invoice reference, amount, PHP currency, and payment mode before accepting its link or payment.</p></li></ol><p>Repeating uncertain creation can produce another session, even with the same reference. If you cannot locate the session, leave the attempt unresolved and reconcile it with the provider. A confirmed failed or expired attempt may offer Start checkout again, or Start test checkout again in test mode, after server validation. Needs review requires operator reconciliation before further payment.</p><p>Records retain their original labels: PayMongo · live, PayMongo · test, or Simulated · paid. An attempt from another mode stays visible, but cannot be opened, recovered, or rechecked under the current mode. Payment confirmation may arrive through a configured webhook; otherwise use Recheck payment.</p><p>Checkout history shows the latest 100 attempts independently of the invoice list. View attempt opens the record, including a recent attempt for an older invoice. Resolve outstanding checkouts before changing plans or canceling renewal. Payments cover one invoice at a time. Card vaulting, automatic renewal debits, and provider refunds are not offered by this screen.</p></div></details>
                    <details><summary>Cancel renewal or understand paused access <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><p><strong>Cancel renewal</strong> keeps access until the stated trial or paid-period end. <strong>Resume renewal</strong> clears cancellation and does not collect payment. Your records are preserved.</p><p>A missed renewal or expired trial has seven days of grace. Afterwards, sales and core catalog/stock changes pause, including new replenishment drafts. Records, permitted returns, exports, order cancellation, and billing stay available. Voluntary cancellation has no extra grace period.</p><p>Plan limits count all locations and active staff, including administrators. Paused staff do not count; reactivation needs room in the plan. Changing a trial plan does not restart the original trial clock.</p></div></details>
                    <a class="text-link" href="{{ route('workspace', ['section' => 'billing']) }}">Open Billing & plans</a>
                </article>

                <article id="channels" class="guide-section" data-search="commerce channels web store marketplace social shop simulation order pending reserved available physical import cancel pause resume sync catalog sku failure retry original quote">
                    <span class="guide-anchor" id="commerce" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    <h2>Sales channels</h2>
                    <p class="section-lead">Managers and administrators can practice catalog sync and online orders locally. No external storefront, webhook, or payment provider is connected.</p>
                    <details open><summary>Add a channel and simulate an order <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Open Channels at the correct location.</strong><p>Select Add channel, enter a name, choose Web store, Marketplace, or Social shop, and confirm the shared stock location. Type and location stay fixed afterwards.</p></li><li><strong>Review Catalog.</strong><p>Central products map by SKU. Healthy local copies publish available units and refresh after catalog edits, stock changes, and reservations.</p></li><li><strong>Select Simulate paid order.</strong><p>Enter a unique External order reference and choose products and quantities. The server saves a quote with current retail prices and tax. Pending units are reserved immediately; physical stock is unchanged.</p></li></ol></div></details>
                    <details><summary>Import or cancel a pending order <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><p>In <strong>Orders → View order</strong>, review the original quote. Select <strong>Import order</strong> to create its sale once. Reserved units are released as the same units leave physical stock; the original prices and tax stay fixed.</p><p>For example, importing 8 reserved units from 10 on hand leaves 2 on hand, zero reserved, and 2 available. It does not deduct twice.</p><p><strong>Cancel pending order</strong> releases availability without reducing physical stock. Imported orders use the sale return workflow in Reports. Sale receipts and exports identify the simulated channel source; these practice payments must not be collected or refunded with real money.</p></div></details>
                    <details><summary>Pause a channel or check its sync history <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><p><strong>Pause channel</strong> publishes zero and stops new orders. Existing pending orders stay reserved; importing or cancelling them is still possible while paused when the action’s subscription access allows it. <strong>Resume channel</strong> refreshes availability and enables new orders.</p><p>Select <strong>Sync catalog</strong> to record a manual snapshot in <strong>Sync history</strong>. Automatic stock/catalog updates do not flood the history. Published counts may be stale, but every order still checks central availability.</p><p>For failure practice, open <strong>Manage channel</strong>, enable <strong>Practice a sync failure</strong>, save, and sync. The previous published copy stays visible with a failed/stale status. Turn that setting off, save, and choose <strong>Retry sync</strong> for a new attempt. Healthy channels continue updating; paused channels stay at zero.</p></div></details>
                    <div class="guide-note"><p><strong>Action interrupted?</strong> Keep the same browser tab and select <strong>Check update status</strong>. Reloading that tab recovers the original request. Do not change it or start the next stage while the result is unknown. A confirmed sync failure needs a new attempt after fixing the setting.</p></div>
                    <p class="section-footnote">The order view includes all pending and the latest 200 handled orders; Sync history shows the latest 200 attempts. Pending reservations protect stock from POS, other channels, transfers, and negative adjustments. After subscription writes pause, records, channel pausing, and pending cancellations remain available; new orders and imports pause.</p>
                    <a class="text-link" href="{{ route('workspace', ['section' => 'channels']) }}">Open Channels</a>
                </article>

                <article id="hardware" class="guide-section" data-search="hardware counter tablet touch screen barcode scanner receipt printer cash drawer card terminal setup keyboard print bluetooth usb">
                    <h2>Counter setup</h2>
                    <div class="hardware-list"><div><h3>Screen & connection</h3><p>Use a computer or tablet with a current browser and a reliable connection. Check the touch layout at your normal screen size.</p></div><div><h3>Barcode scanner</h3><p>Use a scanner in keyboard mode. Pair or connect it, focus the POS search field, and scan a barcode already saved on a product. Check the result before adding it.</p></div><div><h3>Receipt printer</h3><p>Install the manufacturer’s driver and print a test page. Choose the receipt printer in the browser’s print dialog and check the paper size, margins, and scale.</p></div><div><h3>Cash drawer & card terminal</h3><p>Open the drawer manually or through your printer’s own configuration. Process card and contactless payments on a separate approved terminal.</p></div></div>
                    <p class="section-footnote">Direct printer commands, automatic cash-drawer opening, and integrated chip, swipe, or contactless card processing are not included. Hardware compatibility should be tested with your actual devices.</p>
                </article>

                <article id="troubleshooting" class="guide-section" data-search="help troubleshooting error offline connection stock missing scanner barcode printer duplicate sale refresh forgot password reset login email recovery billing ecommerce integration support">
                    <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
                    <details><summary>A sale did not finish or the connection dropped <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><p>Keep the same browser tab open and reconnect. Select <strong>Check sale status</strong> in the payment dialog, or <strong>Check sale</strong> in Point of sale. This retries the original cart and payment: it retrieves the saved receipt, or completes the original checkout once if it was not saved. Reloading the same tab can recover that checkout from browser session storage.</p><p>Do not change or recreate the checkout while its result is unknown, and do not charge the customer's card or wallet again. If POSONLY confirms that no sale was saved and displays a validation error, close the dialog, fix the reported issue, and review the cart before trying again. Offline sales are not supported.</p></div></details>
                    <details><summary>A barcode does not find the product <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><p>Check that the scanner is in keyboard mode and the POS search field has focus. Compare the scanned value with the barcode in Inventory, including leading zeroes. You can search by product name or SKU while your manager corrects the barcode.</p></div></details>
                    <details><summary>The stock figure looks wrong <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><p>Check the selected location first. Count the physical items and review recent sales, receipts, returns, adjustments, and transfers. Ask a manager to enter a reasoned adjustment only after the difference is understood.</p></div></details>
                    <details><summary>A receipt will not print <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><p>Check the printer’s power, connection, paper, and operating-system print queue. Print a test page, then choose the correct printer in the browser dialog. Reopen the saved sale in Reports to print another copy.</p></div></details>
                    <details><summary>I cannot see a button or complete an action <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><p>Read the validation message and check required fields. Your role may not allow the action. If sales or stock changes are paused, ask your administrator to review Billing & plans and its access end date. If the message says operations were paused by the platform administrator, ask the platform owner to review Companies. Existing records stay available.</p></div></details>
                    <details id="password"><summary>I forgot my password or cannot sign in <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Select Forgot password? on the login page.</strong><p>Enter your work email and select Send reset link.</p></li><li><strong>Open the reset email.</strong><p>Follow the link within {{ config('auth.passwords.'.config('auth.defaults.passwords').'.expire') }} minutes. Enter and confirm a new password of at least 10 characters, then select Save new password.</p></li><li><strong>Sign in with your new password.</strong><p>Your previous sessions on other devices are invalidated. Use your own account to continue.</p></li></ol><p>The request message does not confirm whether an account exists or an email was delivered. Check spam and ask your administrator if the email does not arrive. Paused accounts must be reactivated by an administrator. Sample-business accounts cannot use password recovery.</p><a class="text-link" href="{{ route('password.request') }}">Request a reset link</a></div></details>
                    <details><summary>What is connected in this version? <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><p>Sales, product records, retail/wholesale prices, location stock/reservations, simulated channel orders, partial returns, delivery history, replenishment drafts, cash sessions, billing records, customer records, team roles, and business settings use the Laravel database.</p><p>External e-commerce sync/webhooks, offline sales, integrated POS card or wallet charging/refunds, automatic subscription debits, supplier messaging, email invitations, and complete accounting integration are not connected. Held carts stay in this browser/account and reserve no stock. Enabled PayMongo subscription checkout and payment verification contact its API in the configured mode. Live mode collects real payments; test confirmations and local simulations do not. Simulated channel payments contact no provider.</p></div></details>
                    <div class="guide-end"><a href="{{ $workspaceUrl }}">{{ $companySignedIn ? 'Back to workspace' : 'Open your workspace' }}</a></div>
                </article>

                <article id="developer" class="guide-section" data-search="developer portal platform owner admin invite setup private account password company companies pause resume operations subscription invoice reason activity website content cms draft preview publish version">
                    <h2>Developer portal</h2>
                    <p class="section-lead">The developer portal uses its own account and login. Company registration and staff Administrator roles do not grant this access.</p>
                    <details id="developer-setup"><summary>Set up a developer account privately <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><p>The application's operator runs this command locally from the project directory, replacing the example email and name:</p><p><code>php artisan platform:invite owner@example.com --name="Platform owner" --no-interaction</code></p><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Open the generated private file locally.</strong><p>Use storage/app/private/developer-setup.html and follow its setup link within 24 hours.</p></li><li><strong>Choose and confirm a password.</strong><p>Use at least 12 characters. Then sign in at /developer/login; setup does not sign you in automatically.</p></li><li><strong>Delete the private setup file.</strong><p>The link works once. Keep the file and link out of chat, public folders, and shared repositories. No invitation email is sent.</p></li></ol><p>Reinviting a pending account replaces its previous link. An activated account cannot be overwritten this way. Developer and company sessions can coexist; signing out of one preserves the other.</p></div></details>
                    <details><summary>Review company access and billing records <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Open Companies and select View company.</strong><p>Review Company overview, Invoices, or Payment attempts for the business.</p></li><li><strong>Choose the needed action.</strong><p>Pause operations / Resume operations, Change plan, Cancel renewal / Resume renewal, or Create invoice. Review the affected company and enter a reason of 3–300 characters before confirming.</p></li><li><strong>Check the result and Platform activity.</strong><p>Successful changes record the developer, company, reason, and before/after state.</p></li></ol><p>Pausing stops new sales and core stock changes while preserving records, reports, permitted returns, and billing/reconciliation access. It does not reset a trial or change paid-period dates. Resuming removes the platform pause; subscription expiry and plan limits still apply.</p><p>Plans must fit all locations and active staff. Paid-period changes start at the next renewal. Resolve outstanding PayMongo checkouts in the company workspace before a conflicting billing change.</p><p>Subscriptions &amp; invoices separates live PayMongo confirmations, test records, and local simulations. Create invoice adds an open record without collecting payment or activating a paid period. Renewal actions update records without debiting a card. The portal cannot start/recheck provider payments or manually mark an invoice paid; its totals are invoice confirmations, not settlement figures or MRR. Reading lists and company details does not change billing records.</p></div></details>
                    <details id="website-content"><summary>Save, preview, and publish website text <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="detail-content"><p>Open <strong>Website content</strong> and edit the available title, description, hero text, button label, footer tagline, and support email. Select <strong>Save draft</strong> with a reason, then <strong>Preview saved draft</strong>. Select <strong>Publish to website</strong> with a reason to show the saved draft on this running website; it does not deploy the application.</p><p>Save typed changes before publishing. If another session changed the draft, your typed text stays available. Use <strong>Review saved version</strong> and confirm any discard/reload before replacing it. Plan pricing and page layout stay controlled by the application.</p></div></details>
                    <a class="text-link" href="{{ $developerPortalUrl }}">Developer portal</a>
                </article>
            </div>
        </div>
